<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # <span style="color: #d35400;">Robots on Earth and in Space</span> – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> 12th Grade (CEFR: B1.3)</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> Robots on Earth and in Space</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> How Robots Are Used in Panama</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Conduct a presentation on a general skill. Interactive: Participate in debates on abstract concepts.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can conduct a presentation demonstrating a general skill, such as using a new app (e.g., "I will demonstrate how to operate a robotic arm used in Panama's agricultural sector to improve planting efficiency.") Can participate in debates on abstract concepts related to technology (e.g., "Robot technology might replace some human roles, but it's unlikely to fully take over creative or emotional jobs.") Can respond to questions after a presentation, clarifying details of the skill (e.g., "How does this satellite navigation system work?" "This system tracks the spacecraft's position by communicating with multiple satellites and calculating precise coordinates.")</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px; margin-bottom: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #e0f2f7;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher begins by showing a short (1-minute) video clip featuring various robots used in different Panamanian sectors (e.g., agriculture for planting, logistics in the Panama Canal, medical procedures). After the video, the teacher asks students to share their initial reactions or specific robots they recognized. Students are prompted with questions like, "What kind of robots did you see? How do you think they are operated?" to encourage immediate verbal responses and activate prior knowledge about "robots" and "automation".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models sentences using the future perfect, passive voice, and zero conditional, integrating target vocabulary. For example: "By 2030, robots will have transformed Panama's agriculture, making processes more efficient." (Future perfect, efficient, agriculture). "Data is collected by these automated machines for analysis." (Passive voice, automated, machines, collect). "If robots are programmed correctly, they perform tasks with great precision and safety." (Zero conditional, program, safety, precision). The teacher explicitly models the pronunciation of /r/ in "robots" and /s/ in "space" and the -ed endings for "controlled" (/d/), "launched" (/t/), and "operated" (/ɪd/), ensuring clear articulation. Students repeat after the teacher.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the modeled structures and vocabulary by asking:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say, 'By next year, this factory will have implemented new robotic equipment,' am I talking about something that happened in the past, now, or in the future?" (Future)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say, 'The new satellite was launched into orbit,' who launched it? Do I know the specific person or is the action more important?" (The action/satellite, not the specific person.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What sound do you hear at the end of 'operated'? Is it /d/, /t/, or /ɪd/?" (/ɪd/)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Robots on Earth and in Space"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An audio recording of a brief interview (2-3 minutes) with a Panamanian expert discussing the current and future applications of robotics in local industries (e.g., the use of robotic arms in agriculture, drones for surveying land, or remote-controlled submarines for marine research). The interview uses informal yet informative register, providing concrete examples related to the theme "How Robots Are Used in Panama" and includes target vocabulary like "agriculture," "research," "control," "operate," "efficient," and "automation." It also naturally incorporates future perfect and passive voice structures.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Participating in discussions on the use of robotics in various fields, offering solutions in group discussions about robotics, and encouraging respectful dialogue on diverse perspectives regarding the impacts of robotics.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students listen to the interview twice. After the first listen, they answer gist questions orally in pairs:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What main industries in Panama are mentioned as using robots?" (e.g., agriculture, marine research)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is the expert's general opinion about the future of robotics in Panama?" (e.g., positive, transformative)</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">After the second listen, students work in small groups to discuss and verbally complete True/False statements the teacher provides on the board, justifying their answers with details from the audio. E.g., "True or False: By 2025, most Panamanian farms will have fully automated their planting process." (False, the expert predicts significant transformation but not full automation by 2025).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice using the future perfect, passive voice, and zero conditional orally, while integrating vocabulary related to robotics and space exploration. Focus on clear pronunciation of /r/, /s/, and -ed endings.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice (Sentence Completion):</b> The teacher provides sentence beginnings on a slide, and students complete them orally in pairs, focusing on the target grammar structures and vocabulary. Each pair takes turns saying their completed sentences. E.g., "By the end of the decade, robotic technology..." (will have improved agricultural efficiency). "Many new discoveries are..." (collected by space telescopes). "If a sensor detects an anomaly..." (the robot stops operating). The teacher monitors pronunciation of target sounds and -ed endings.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice (Role-Play Scenario):</b> Students work in groups of three. The teacher gives them a specific scenario: "You are a team of engineers discussing the next steps for a new robotic project in Panama (e.g., a robotic arm for fruit harvesting or a drone for jungle exploration). Discuss the impacts by 2025, how it is controlled, and potential safety measures." Students take turns suggesting sentences using future perfect, passive voice, and zero conditional. Example prompts: "What will our robot *have achieved* by 2025?" "How *is* the data *collected*?" "What happens *if* a problem *occurs*?" The teacher provides immediate feedback on grammar and pronunciation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction (Picture Description & Questioning):</b> The teacher displays various images of robots used in Panama or space (e.g., a robot in a hospital, a satellite orbiting Earth, an agricultural drone). In pairs, students describe what they see using passive voice ("The robot is operated by...") and speculate about future impacts using future perfect ("By 2030, this technology will have revolutionized..."). They then ask each other cause-effect questions using the zero conditional ("If this robot malfunctions, what happens?"). Students are encouraged to use vocabulary like "operate," "collect," "explore," "research," "efficient," "safety," "control," "mission," "discovery."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Robots on Earth and in Space"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Group Debate and Presentation. Students are divided into two teams for a debate on the abstract concept: "Robot technology might replace some human roles, but it's unlikely to fully take over creative or emotional jobs." (Team A: Argues for replacement; Team B: Argues against full takeover). Each team has 5 minutes to prepare their arguments, ensuring they incorporate the future perfect, passive voice, zero conditional, and target vocabulary. After the debate, each team designates a speaker to present a summary of their main arguments, concluding with a suggested solution or a balanced perspective, ensuring all viewpoints are considered (Sociolinguistic competence).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme and Scenario:</b> The debate directly explores the implications of "Robots on Earth" specifically in human roles, extending to the "abstract concepts related to technology" mentioned in the specific objective. During the presentations, students are encouraged to reference specific examples of "How Robots Are Used in Panama" (e.g., in "agriculture" or "industry") and discuss their future impact. Students must also practice encouraging respectful dialogue on diverse perspectives regarding the impacts of robotics (Pragmatic competence).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency, collaborative interaction, effective communication of arguments, and appropriate use of target grammar and vocabulary. The teacher will circulate, listening for the correct use of structures, vocabulary (e.g., "automation," "efficiency," "control," "safety"), and pronunciation points (/r/, /s/, -ed endings).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on speaking fluency, accuracy of target grammar (future perfect, passive voice, zero conditional), and appropriate use of vocabulary. Peer assessment using a simplified rubric for clarity of arguments and participation in respectful dialogue.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students participate in the group debate and deliver their summary presentation. The teacher observes and takes notes on individual and group performance. Peers provide feedback based on a rubric that asks: "Did your group member use future predictions (e.g., 'will have transformed')?" "Did they use language to describe how things are done (e.g., 'is collected by')?" "Did they speak clearly, pronouncing 'robots' and 'space' well?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible Pronunciation:</b> Clear articulation of /r/, /s/, and -ed endings (controlled, launched, operated).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Integration of at least 5-7 key vocabulary words (e.g., technology, automation, industry, efficient, control, operate, safety, exploration).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar Accuracy:</b> Correct application of future perfect, passive voice, and zero conditional.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and Cohesion:</b> Ability to present arguments clearly and logically, responding to questions after the debate.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration & Pragmatic/Sociolinguistic Skills:</b> Active participation, encouraging respectful dialogue, and offering solutions in group discussions.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new thing you learned or clarified about how robots are used in Panama or space today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do you feel now using the future perfect to talk about robotic impacts, or the passive voice to describe robot functions?" (Scale 1-5)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-8 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What specific strategies did you use today to make your speaking more persuasive during the debate or clear during your presentation?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What aspect of speaking about robotics (vocabulary, grammar, pronunciation) do you want to improve in the next lesson?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The debate format successfully engaged most students, fostering rich discussion and use of target grammar. Students demonstrated good control of /r/ and /s/, but some still struggled with consistent -ed endings (/ɪd/ vs. /d/ vs. /t/). I need to prepare more focused pronunciation drills for -ed endings in the next lesson, perhaps through minimal pairs or tongue twisters that incorporate the target sounds. I will also ensure specific feedback on pragmatic aspects like encouraging respectful dialogue is consistently given.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 4, students will transition to writing. They will apply the vocabulary and grammatical structures (future perfect, passive voice, zero conditional) practiced in this speaking lesson to compose a short argumentative essay or a blog post discussing the pros and cons of robotics in a specific Panamanian industry or the ethical considerations of space exploration, preparing them to construct sentences and paragraphs using the target vocabulary for the writing focus.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>